1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,580
You're desperately thirsty,

2
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:03,606
and you get to pick one of two cups.

3
00:00:03,662 --> 00:00:05,088
One is muddy brown.

4
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:06,587
The other is crystal clear.

5
00:00:07,227 --> 00:00:09,508
Most people grab the clear one right away.

6
00:00:09,565 --> 00:00:10,745
But in the real world,

7
00:00:10,765 --> 00:00:12,307
clear water can make you just

8
00:00:12,327 --> 00:00:13,877
as sick as muddy water.

9
00:00:14,517 --> 00:00:15,696
Because the most dangerous

10
00:00:15,716 --> 00:00:17,297
things in water are usually

11
00:00:17,317 --> 00:00:18,967
the things your eyes can't see.

12
00:00:19,607 --> 00:00:22,276
Today, Zero has to find drinking water.

13
00:00:22,332 --> 00:00:23,557
And he has to choose right.

14
00:00:25,157 --> 00:00:27,624
Yesterday, Zero explored the valley,

15
00:00:27,644 --> 00:00:29,186
drew a map in the dirt,

16
00:00:29,206 --> 00:00:31,407
and picked the site for his first base.

17
00:00:32,047 --> 00:00:33,053
But the whole day,

18
00:00:33,084 --> 00:00:34,497
he didn't drink a single drop.

19
00:00:35,137 --> 00:00:36,318
Morning of day two.

20
00:00:36,374 --> 00:00:37,550
A dry mouth,

21
00:00:37,587 --> 00:00:38,680
a slight headache,

22
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:39,649
dark urine.

23
00:00:39,712 --> 00:00:41,230
The dehydration warning signs

24
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:43,300
he learned yesterday are showing up.

25
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:45,595
Today's goal: find the least

26
00:00:45,615 --> 00:00:47,158
risky source of water,

27
00:00:47,190 --> 00:00:49,153
and find a way to get it up to his mouth.

28
00:00:49,793 --> 00:00:51,774
The catch: no fire yet.

29
00:00:51,831 --> 00:00:52,675
No pot.

30
00:00:52,731 --> 00:00:53,737
No container.

31
00:00:53,793 --> 00:00:54,936
Just two hands,

32
00:00:54,956 --> 00:00:56,155
and a map on the ground.

33
00:00:56,795 --> 00:00:59,250
So today, Zero can't boil water.

34
00:00:59,307 --> 00:01:00,412
All he can do is choose

35
00:01:00,432 --> 00:01:02,457
the source as smartly as possible.

36
00:01:03,657 --> 00:01:05,208
Zero studies his map.

37
00:01:05,270 --> 00:01:06,250
In this valley,

38
00:01:06,270 --> 00:01:08,382
there are five kinds of places with water.

39
00:01:09,022 --> 00:01:10,773
The wide, slow river.

40
00:01:10,835 --> 00:01:12,091
The fast rapids.

41
00:01:12,147 --> 00:01:13,966
Muddy puddles on the clay bank.

42
00:01:14,022 --> 00:01:15,691
A still pool in the jungle.

43
00:01:15,747 --> 00:01:17,134
And the waterfall upstream.

44
00:01:17,774 --> 00:01:19,211
But before he sets off,

45
00:01:19,261 --> 00:01:21,180
he needs something to scoop water with.

46
00:01:21,236 --> 00:01:23,623
Zero picks a big leaf at the jungle edge.

47
00:01:24,263 --> 00:01:25,663
He folds it into a cone

48
00:01:25,688 --> 00:01:27,000
and pinches the tip.

49
00:01:27,063 --> 00:01:28,242
With nothing to cut it,

50
00:01:28,262 --> 00:01:30,388
the edge tears and goes ragged.

51
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:31,212
But it works.

52
00:01:31,852 --> 00:01:33,770
The closest stop is the river.

53
00:01:33,826 --> 00:01:35,951
Zero scoops some water up to take a look.

54
00:01:36,591 --> 00:01:37,683
It's fairly clear,

55
00:01:37,703 --> 00:01:38,867
with no smell.

56
00:01:38,929 --> 00:01:40,216
But here's the problem:

57
00:01:40,341 --> 00:01:41,946
how do you know it's clean enough,

58
00:01:41,966 --> 00:01:43,428
with no way to measure anything?

59
00:01:44,068 --> 00:01:45,848
All Zero has are his eyes,

60
00:01:45,868 --> 00:01:47,736
his nose, and an understanding

61
00:01:47,756 --> 00:01:49,480
of where dirt in water comes from.

62
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:52,564
Let's magnify a single drop of water.

63
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:53,625
What's mixed into water

64
00:01:53,645 --> 00:01:55,408
falls into two big groups.

65
00:01:56,048 --> 00:01:57,710
The first group is sediment:

66
00:01:57,735 --> 00:02:00,573
soil, sand, and bits of leaves.

67
00:02:00,635 --> 00:02:02,215
That's what makes water cloudy,

68
00:02:02,235 --> 00:02:03,222
and we can see it.

69
00:02:03,862 --> 00:02:06,167
The second group is tiny living things,

70
00:02:06,187 --> 00:02:08,154
like bacteria, viruses,

71
00:02:08,174 --> 00:02:09,480
and some parasites.

72
00:02:09,536 --> 00:02:11,154
Some of them cause diarrhea,

73
00:02:11,174 --> 00:02:12,362
and serious illness.

74
00:02:13,002 --> 00:02:15,081
The smallest thing the human eye can see

75
00:02:15,101 --> 00:02:17,008
is about a tenth of a millimeter.

76
00:02:17,064 --> 00:02:18,551
Roughly the thickness of a hair.

77
00:02:19,191 --> 00:02:20,895
But most bacteria are only about

78
00:02:20,915 --> 00:02:22,572
a thousandth of a millimeter.

79
00:02:22,628 --> 00:02:24,403
Dozens of times thinner than a hair.

80
00:02:25,043 --> 00:02:27,137
And viruses are much smaller still.

81
00:02:27,193 --> 00:02:28,975
Even with lots of germs in it,

82
00:02:29,018 --> 00:02:31,168
water can still look perfectly clear.

83
00:02:31,808 --> 00:02:33,600
That's why clear water only tells

84
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:35,064
you there's little sediment.

85
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:37,007
It does not tell you the water is safe.

86
00:02:38,207 --> 00:02:40,100
Even today, around the world,

87
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:42,175
waterborne diseases still make huge

88
00:02:42,195 --> 00:02:44,294
numbers of people sick every year.

89
00:02:44,934 --> 00:02:46,653
So where do these germs come from?

90
00:02:46,709 --> 00:02:49,364
Mostly from human and animal droppings,

91
00:02:49,384 --> 00:02:51,301
dead animals, and dirty water

92
00:02:51,321 --> 00:02:53,371
draining into rivers and ponds.

93
00:02:54,011 --> 00:02:55,761
Animals that drink from a stream

94
00:02:55,811 --> 00:02:58,061
or cross it can leave germs behind.

95
00:02:58,124 --> 00:02:59,973
Then the current carries them downstream.

96
00:03:00,613 --> 00:03:01,555
And in warm,

97
00:03:01,575 --> 00:03:03,317
still water, some germs

98
00:03:03,337 --> 00:03:04,880
can multiply easily,

99
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:06,700
because nothing carries them away.

100
00:03:07,340 --> 00:03:09,157
So Zero comes up with four simple

101
00:03:09,177 --> 00:03:11,315
questions to compare water sources.

102
00:03:11,955 --> 00:03:13,410
One: is it flowing,

103
00:03:13,430 --> 00:03:14,268
or still?

104
00:03:14,330 --> 00:03:15,561
Two: what's upstream?

105
00:03:15,630 --> 00:03:17,806
Three: are there signs of animals?

106
00:03:17,868 --> 00:03:19,435
And four: color,

107
00:03:19,455 --> 00:03:21,567
smell, and anything floating in it.

108
00:03:22,207 --> 00:03:23,112
These questions don't

109
00:03:23,132 --> 00:03:24,944
make water any safer.

110
00:03:25,007 --> 00:03:25,762
But they help you pick

111
00:03:25,782 --> 00:03:27,257
the source that's less risky.

112
00:03:28,457 --> 00:03:29,488
Back at the river.

113
00:03:29,544 --> 00:03:30,994
The water keeps flowing.

114
00:03:31,056 --> 00:03:32,107
That's a pass.

115
00:03:32,169 --> 00:03:33,707
But then Zero looks upstream.

116
00:03:34,347 --> 00:03:36,127
He sees tracks from several animals

117
00:03:36,147 --> 00:03:38,234
coming down to the water's edge.

118
00:03:38,297 --> 00:03:39,752
This stretch of river is where

119
00:03:39,772 --> 00:03:41,022
animals come to drink.

120
00:03:41,662 --> 00:03:43,419
So the river gets a middle score.

121
00:03:43,475 --> 00:03:45,342
Usable if there's no other choice,

122
00:03:45,362 --> 00:03:46,837
but not the best source.

123
00:03:47,637 --> 00:03:50,362
Second stop: a puddle by the clay bank.

124
00:03:50,424 --> 00:03:52,255
The water is murky brown,

125
00:03:52,311 --> 00:03:53,517
warm from the sun,

126
00:03:53,537 --> 00:03:54,536
and going nowhere.

127
00:03:55,176 --> 00:03:56,739
Zero dips his leaf in.

128
00:03:56,801 --> 00:03:58,456
The water is full of sediment,

129
00:03:58,476 --> 00:04:00,288
and it smells strongly of mud.

130
00:04:00,928 --> 00:04:02,258
Leave it, and the sediment

131
00:04:02,278 --> 00:04:03,678
will sink to the bottom.

132
00:04:03,741 --> 00:04:05,159
But the germs in warm,

133
00:04:05,190 --> 00:04:07,241
still water aren't going anywhere.

134
00:04:07,881 --> 00:04:10,393
This puddle fails almost every test.

135
00:04:10,456 --> 00:04:12,549
Still, warm, and murky.

136
00:04:12,606 --> 00:04:14,019
Zero doesn't drink a drop.

137
00:04:14,659 --> 00:04:16,483
Third stop: the rapids.

138
00:04:16,546 --> 00:04:17,939
The water is strong,

139
00:04:17,959 --> 00:04:19,134
and constantly churning.

140
00:04:19,774 --> 00:04:21,816
But yesterday, Zero already noted

141
00:04:21,836 --> 00:04:23,549
that the rocks here are slippery

142
00:04:23,574 --> 00:04:25,342
and the current is dangerous.

143
00:04:25,398 --> 00:04:26,724
Scooping water here,

144
00:04:26,761 --> 00:04:27,841
he's more likely to fall

145
00:04:27,861 --> 00:04:28,961
in than to get sick.

146
00:04:29,601 --> 00:04:31,782
Fourth stop: deep in the jungle,

147
00:04:31,813 --> 00:04:34,364
Zero finds a small pool under the trees.

148
00:04:35,004 --> 00:04:36,284
The water is so clear you

149
00:04:36,304 --> 00:04:38,047
can see the leaves on the bottom.

150
00:04:38,103 --> 00:04:39,459
It's cooler than the river,

151
00:04:39,479 --> 00:04:40,703
and perfectly calm.

152
00:04:41,343 --> 00:04:42,349
No sediment.

153
00:04:42,406 --> 00:04:43,312
No smell.

154
00:04:43,368 --> 00:04:44,799
Judging by eye alone,

155
00:04:44,843 --> 00:04:46,123
this pool beats every

156
00:04:46,143 --> 00:04:47,781
source he's seen all day.

157
00:04:48,421 --> 00:04:49,852
Zero kneels down,

158
00:04:49,883 --> 00:04:51,214
fills his leaf cup,

159
00:04:51,234 --> 00:04:52,583
and lifts it up to drink.

160
00:04:53,223 --> 00:04:55,473
But before the water reaches his mouth,

161
00:04:55,498 --> 00:04:57,210
he sees something moving in it.

162
00:04:57,850 --> 00:05:00,255
Tiny things, wriggling up and down,

163
00:05:00,275 --> 00:05:01,106
rhythmically.

164
00:05:01,163 --> 00:05:02,475
These are mosquito larvae.

165
00:05:03,115 --> 00:05:05,482
Zero pours the water out immediately,

166
00:05:05,502 --> 00:05:07,265
and looks around the edge of the pool.

167
00:05:07,905 --> 00:05:09,022
The mud around the pool

168
00:05:09,042 --> 00:05:11,017
is covered in animal tracks.

169
00:05:11,067 --> 00:05:12,479
And there are droppings nearby.

170
00:05:13,119 --> 00:05:14,475
No water flows in.

171
00:05:14,531 --> 00:05:15,938
No water flows out.

172
00:05:15,994 --> 00:05:17,161
Animals from all over

173
00:05:17,181 --> 00:05:18,619
the jungle drink here,

174
00:05:18,669 --> 00:05:20,124
and everything that goes in,

175
00:05:20,144 --> 00:05:20,894
stays in.

176
00:05:21,534 --> 00:05:22,577
On top of that,

177
00:05:22,621 --> 00:05:24,051
leaves that fall in slowly

178
00:05:24,071 --> 00:05:24,996
rot on the bottom.

179
00:05:25,636 --> 00:05:27,128
The larvae tell us this water

180
00:05:27,148 --> 00:05:28,740
has been still for days,

181
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:30,441
because mosquitoes love to lay

182
00:05:30,461 --> 00:05:32,260
their eggs in standing water.

183
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:34,038
And the clearness?

184
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:35,068
That's just sediment

185
00:05:35,088 --> 00:05:36,456
settling to the bottom.

186
00:05:36,512 --> 00:05:37,817
The invisible germs are

187
00:05:37,837 --> 00:05:39,475
all still floating around.

188
00:05:40,115 --> 00:05:41,332
The clearest water of all

189
00:05:41,352 --> 00:05:43,065
turned out to fail the hardest.

190
00:05:43,705 --> 00:05:45,310
Today's lesson: clear water

191
00:05:45,330 --> 00:05:46,893
does not mean clean water.

192
00:05:48,293 --> 00:05:50,512
Zero goes back over his questions.

193
00:05:50,568 --> 00:05:52,611
Low-risk water has to keep flowing,

194
00:05:52,631 --> 00:05:54,011
and whatever is upstream has

195
00:05:54,031 --> 00:05:55,668
to be as clean as possible.

196
00:05:56,308 --> 00:05:58,239
That means going as high as he can.

197
00:05:58,295 --> 00:05:58,945
Upstream.

198
00:05:59,585 --> 00:06:00,789
He follows the river all

199
00:06:00,809 --> 00:06:02,402
the way up to the waterfall,

200
00:06:02,422 --> 00:06:03,522
at the head of the valley.

201
00:06:04,162 --> 00:06:05,254
Even the waterfall gets

202
00:06:05,274 --> 00:06:07,392
its water from a stream up above,

203
00:06:07,412 --> 00:06:09,161
where we can't see what's there.

204
00:06:09,224 --> 00:06:11,175
But then Zero notices something else.

205
00:06:11,815 --> 00:06:13,609
On the rock wall beside him,

206
00:06:13,653 --> 00:06:14,708
water is seeping out

207
00:06:14,728 --> 00:06:16,246
of a crack in the stone.

208
00:06:16,303 --> 00:06:16,921
Drip.

209
00:06:16,978 --> 00:06:17,578
By drip.

210
00:06:18,193 --> 00:06:19,424
This is groundwater.

211
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:20,898
Water that has soaked down through

212
00:06:20,918 --> 00:06:23,093
soil and rock for a long time.

213
00:06:23,733 --> 00:06:24,525
On the way,

214
00:06:24,545 --> 00:06:26,225
the soil and rock filter out

215
00:06:26,245 --> 00:06:28,409
sediment and some of the germs.

216
00:06:28,471 --> 00:06:30,150
So this kind of water is often

217
00:06:30,170 --> 00:06:31,958
less risky than surface water.

218
00:06:32,598 --> 00:06:33,666
But not always.

219
00:06:33,723 --> 00:06:36,004
Groundwater can be contaminated too,

220
00:06:36,047 --> 00:06:38,485
if dirt and waste soak down into it.

221
00:06:39,125 --> 00:06:40,930
The water dripping here never ran

222
00:06:40,950 --> 00:06:42,963
across ground where animals walk.

223
00:06:43,603 --> 00:06:46,122
Zero sets his leaf cup under the drip,

224
00:06:46,166 --> 00:06:47,158
careful not to let

225
00:06:47,178 --> 00:06:48,683
the leaf touch the moss,

226
00:06:48,703 --> 00:06:49,491
or the ground.

227
00:06:50,131 --> 00:06:52,073
Because however good the source is,

228
00:06:52,093 --> 00:06:53,611
if the container is dirty,

229
00:06:53,631 --> 00:06:55,069
the water gets dirty too.

230
00:06:55,709 --> 00:06:56,689
After a while,

231
00:06:56,709 --> 00:06:57,915
the cup fills up.

232
00:06:57,971 --> 00:06:59,684
Clear, no smell,

233
00:06:59,721 --> 00:07:01,571
and from a place animals can't reach.

234
00:07:02,211 --> 00:07:04,480
The torn leaf cup still leaks a little.

235
00:07:04,536 --> 00:07:06,674
Zero has to hold it with both hands.

236
00:07:07,314 --> 00:07:08,882
Time for the four questions.

237
00:07:08,939 --> 00:07:10,014
Always flowing.

238
00:07:10,076 --> 00:07:11,657
The source is solid rock.

239
00:07:11,714 --> 00:07:13,057
No animal signs.

240
00:07:13,113 --> 00:07:14,945
No color, no smell.

241
00:07:15,001 --> 00:07:16,026
It passes them all.

242
00:07:16,666 --> 00:07:18,433
Zero is out of options,

243
00:07:18,453 --> 00:07:20,160
so he takes one small sip.

244
00:07:20,216 --> 00:07:22,546
It's the least risky choice he has today,

245
00:07:22,566 --> 00:07:24,291
but it is still not safe.

246
00:07:24,366 --> 00:07:25,529
Please don't copy this.

247
00:07:26,169 --> 00:07:27,212
In real life,

248
00:07:27,232 --> 00:07:29,131
always boil natural water,

249
00:07:29,169 --> 00:07:30,874
or treat it with a proven method,

250
00:07:30,894 --> 00:07:31,907
before you drink it.

251
00:07:32,547 --> 00:07:35,209
And when Zero has fire and a pot one day,

252
00:07:35,259 --> 00:07:36,685
that's the first thing he'll do.

253
00:07:37,325 --> 00:07:40,068
Zero also notes one more source:

254
00:07:40,125 --> 00:07:41,505
rain, caught straight

255
00:07:41,525 --> 00:07:43,367
from the sky on a clean leaf,

256
00:07:43,387 --> 00:07:45,237
before it runs over the ground.

257
00:07:45,300 --> 00:07:46,338
That's low risk too.

258
00:07:46,978 --> 00:07:48,558
Zero heads back to base,

259
00:07:48,578 --> 00:07:50,265
and adds what he learned to his map.

260
00:07:50,905 --> 00:07:52,130
Drips from the rock:

261
00:07:52,180 --> 00:07:54,817
lowest risk, but it still needs boiling.

262
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:57,124
The river: only when necessary.

263
00:07:57,180 --> 00:07:59,249
The rapids: too dangerous.

264
00:07:59,305 --> 00:08:01,498
And the still pool and the mud puddle:

265
00:08:01,555 --> 00:08:02,330
never drink.

266
00:08:02,970 --> 00:08:04,301
So, today's lesson.

267
00:08:04,358 --> 00:08:06,757
Clearness only tells you about sediment.

268
00:08:06,820 --> 00:08:08,332
What tells you more about the risk

269
00:08:08,357 --> 00:08:09,912
is where the water came from,

270
00:08:09,932 --> 00:08:10,970
and what it passed through.

271
00:08:11,610 --> 00:08:13,802
Unlocked: a water source map,

272
00:08:13,822 --> 00:08:15,673
and four questions for choosing water.

273
00:08:17,073 --> 00:08:17,985
But all day,

274
00:08:18,035 --> 00:08:20,103
Zero had to tear leaves by hand.

275
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:21,440
The edges are ragged,

276
00:08:21,460 --> 00:08:23,023
and water keeps leaking out.

277
00:08:23,663 --> 00:08:25,044
With one sharp thing,

278
00:08:25,088 --> 00:08:27,038
everything would be so much easier.

279
00:08:27,100 --> 00:08:28,968
But where do you find something sharp,

280
00:08:28,988 --> 00:08:30,875
in a world with nothing but rocks?

281
00:08:31,515 --> 00:08:33,870
Next time, Zero turns an ordinary

282
00:08:33,890 --> 00:08:36,065
rock into a sharp stone flake.

